#X text 562 293 Lists are represented with square brackets [] \, and lists can contain other lists \, allowing arbitrary levels of nesting. For example: "name": "rhythm" \, "data": [["bd" \, "sd"] \, ["bd" \, ["hh" \, "hh"]]] . Values can be strings \, numbers \, or nested lists. Strings are enclosed in double quotes \, while numbers are written directly without quotes. Nested lists preserve their structure \, so the brackets determine the hierarchy of the data.;
#X text 57 25 The object adopts JSON syntax \, so it uses curly braces to define a dictionay object. The data is a key-value pair and the must be a symbol inside quotes followed a colon - so something like "keyname": - and the value can be an atom \, a list \, a nested list or another dictionary. The key-value pairs must be separated by commas \, which need to be escaped in Pd by a backslash.;
#X text 57 110 The data can be sent as a list \, but you can join curlies \, colons \, commas if you wish \, and make it as compact as a single symbol (which is a one length list)., f 59;
